<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Padmini</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Rani Padmini</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Padmini, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pawaya">Padmavati</a>, is a legendary queen of Chittor, wife of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ratnasimha">Ratnasimha</a>, said to have committed jauhar with the women of the fort when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-khalji">Alauddin Khalji</a> captured it in 1303. Her story first appears in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-muhammad-jayasi">Malik Muhammad Jayasi</a>'s poem <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padmavat">Padmavat</a> of 1540, more than two centuries after the siege, and was elaborated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> bardic tradition. Most historians regard her as a literary rather than a historical figure.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/padmini.jpg" alt="Padmini" width="320" height="401" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:22Princess_Padmavati_ca._1765_Biblioth%C3%A8que_nationale_de_France,_Paris.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
